1. Method: exact Hungarian / Kuhn-Munkres in shortest-augmenting-path form with float64 row/column duals (JV family; no epsilon, no iteration cap). Custom CUDA kernel (src/yolo_kalman/assign.py): one block per stream, 256 threads compact the padded [S,M,N] problem into shared memory, one warp runs the Dijkstra/augment loop. Torch custom op yolo_kalman::lap_batch, CUDA-graph capturable and torch.compile-traceable.
2. lap semantics kept: extend_cost + cost_limit = min sum(c - limit) over matched pairs, solved as n x (m + n) with zero-cost dummy columns; c > limit is never matched, c == limit is matched (closer to lapjv than strict, measured); ties go to the lowest column index (deterministic).
3. Whole cascade on GPU (assign="gpu_hungarian", default): S1-S5, births, id recovery and all bookkeeping as GPU tensors, 3 CUDA graphs per step, one host sync per 8-frame chunk. assign="lapjv" keeps the old CPU cascade as reference; --assign flag in track.py, bench.py and eval_synth.py.
4. Exactness evidence: 7 solver tests, total cost == scipy linear_sum_assignment and == lapjv (random rectangular, empty, 1xN, Nx1, all-gated, at-limit, ties, 3e38/fp16-max, NaN/inf, mixed-size 32-stream batch, graph replay). Real-clip replay: equal total cost in 8,536/8,536 problems, 222 are tie alternatives.
5. Tracking outcomes: GPU cascade is frame-identical to lapjv on the synthetic test scenes; eval_synth tables are identical for both solvers and to the previous results (13/13 ids kept through full occlusion, 0 ID switches). 24/24 pytest pass (14 old + 10 new).
6. Speed end to end, 2 GPUs, gpu_hungarian vs lapjv (same code otherwise): 1,233/883 fps (1 stream), 3,688/2,448 (4), 4,545/2,879 (8), 5,270/3,164 (16), 5,702/3,870 (32); NVML util 45-92% vs 41-68%. The previous README had 3,015 (16) / 3,519 (32).
7. Tracker alone (8 streams): 0.40 ms/step vs 2.09 ms (lapjv). The kernel takes ~7.5 us per launch in-graph; on sparse tracking-like costs one launch for all streams beats per-stream lapjv 3-11x. A pure-PyTorch batched variant was ~300x slower (host sync per Dijkstra step): measured, rejected, kept as CPU fallback.
8. Iterations: fusable element-wise KF (no batched 8x8 matmuls, 218 -> 178 kernels/step), shared-memory staging in the solver (20 -> 7.5 us), producer split into reader + detector threads. Now ~80-86% of the TRT-only ceiling; the remaining limiter is GPU contention (TRT saturates the SMs; a tracker step goes from 0.4 ms alone to ~2.4 ms under load).
9. Found/fixed along the way: an NVDEC copy-out race exposed by the sync-free reader (corrupted frames in the no-re-ID eval until fixed), and the old "benchmark hang", which was a lost multiprocessing result message (the worker now flushes its queue; the parent fails fast).
10. What failed / open: the first full grid hung 3x before the hang fix; after it, 3 first attempts failed fast (rc=1) with logs overwritten, so the root cause is not recorded (the next 23 runs passed). The pipeline is not bit-deterministic run to run, so real-clip id counts vary 601-619 for lapjv alone. No fused single-kernel cascade. Nothing pushed.
